WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.476
The API attack surface includes more than
public internet endpoints.

2
00:00:06.556 --> 00:00:12.532
Public APIs, partner APIs, internal APIs,
service-to-service APIs,

3
00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:19.703
mobile-backed APIs, browser-backed APIs,
third-party integrations, webhooks,

4
00:00:19.783 --> 00:00:26.772
API gateways, and reverse proxies all
create places where requests cross from one

5
00:00:26.852 --> 00:00:29.080
trust level into another.

6
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.692
Internal APIs may feel safer,

7
00:00:31.772 --> 00:00:39.065
but internal callers can be compromised,
misconfigured, outdated, or simply wrong.

8
00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:43.400
Every request has multiple surfaces to
consider.

9
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:50.367
Paths, route parameters, query parameters,
headers, cookies, request bodies,

10
00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:55.613
tokens, and response fields can all carry
security meaning.

11
00:00:55.693 --> 00:00:59.340
A route parameter may identify a resource.

12
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.978
A header may choose a tenant, locale, or
content type.

13
00:01:04.058 --> 00:01:07.603
A body may carry fields that update state.

14
00:01:07.683 --> 00:01:11.329
A response may reveal sensitive metadata.

15
00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:17.284
Security review should follow the entire
request and response path,

16
00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:20.302
not just the obvious JSON payload.

17
00:01:20.382 --> 00:01:26.459
API gateways and reverse proxies can help
centralize authentication,

18
00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:33.731
routing, throttling, and logging, but they
do not remove endpoint responsibility.

19
00:01:33.811 --> 00:01:38.268
The backend service still needs to
validate inputs,

20
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:44.931
enforce authorization for the operation,
and return only appropriate data.

21
00:01:45.011 --> 00:01:48.354
A gateway may know that a token is valid,

22
00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:55.220
but the application usually knows whether
that caller can access this invoice,

23
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.352
change this account, or approve this
workflow.

24
00:01:59.432 --> 00:02:04.598
Trust boundaries should be documented as
part of API design.

25
00:02:04.678 --> 00:02:07.514
Where does untrusted data enter.

26
00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:11.342
Which component authenticates the caller.

27
00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:15.980
Which component authorizes the object and
function.

28
00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.605
Which component validates the contract.

29
00:02:19.685 --> 00:02:21.711
Which logs are created.

30
00:02:21.791 --> 00:02:24.627
When these boundaries are clear,

31
00:02:24.707 --> 00:02:30.379
teams can avoid relying on assumptions
such as hidden UI controls,

32
00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:35.220
friendly internal callers, or network
location alone.
